    OEO:based_on_sector_division OEO:OEO_00010056 ;
    OEO:has_study_keyword "Greenhouse gas emissions",
        "carbon neutrality",
        "decarbonization pathways",
        "policies and measures" ;
    DC:abstract """The "Climate-neutral Germany 2050" study published in 2020 is based on the target set by the German Bundestag and asks how the path to climate neutrality by 2050 can be described in an economically viable strategy. Climate-neutral means that greenhouse gas emissions are completely or almost completely avoided in all areas and the residual emissions are offset by negative emissions, i.e. the removal of CO2 from the atmosphere and subsequent storage.

With the new "Climate-neutral Germany 2045" scenario, we answer the question of whether greenhouse gas neutrality is possible as early as 2045 - with a "yes". This study also describes a strategy that respects investment cycles and does not involve politically prescribed changes in behaviour. The current study shows that, based on the already very ambitious target of a 65 per cent reduction in greenhouse gases by 2030, an accelerated transformation is subsequently possible and necessary in all sectors.

Achieving climate neutrality by 2045 has two decisive advantages: Firstly, it reduces the total emissions still caused by Germany by 2050 and thus the use of the global emissions budget by 900 million tonnes of CO2 equivalents. Secondly, as the largest GHG emitter and largest economy in Europe, Germany can become a lead market and exporter of these technologies by ambitiously ramping up climate protection technologies.""" ;
    DC:acronym "Agora-2045" .

