        OEO:OEO_00020407 ;
    OEO:based_on_sector_division OEO:OEO_00010056 ;
    OEO:has_model <http://openenergy-platform.org/ontology/oekg/models/34>,
        <http://openenergy-platform.org/ontology/oekg/models/41>,
        <http://openenergy-platform.org/ontology/oekg/models/52> ;
    OEO:has_study_keyword "Greenhouse gas emissions",
        "carbon neutrality",
        "decarbonization pathways",
        "degree of electrifiaction",
        "model intercomparison study",
        "negative emissions",
        "policies and measures",
        "scenario projection comparison" ;
    DC:abstract "For the first time, this scenario analysis for Germany presents concrete transformation paths to climate neutrality in 2045 on the basis of a comprehensive model comparison. What is special about this study by the Ariadne project is that six overall system and sector models have been integrated into one study, each of which complements the other in terms of their respective strengths: For specific questions, the model that most accurately depicts the relevant aspects was emphasised as the lead model in each case. Other models were used to analyse the effects of the transformation on environmental protection goods and the distribution of costs across different income groups. This broad-based approach makes it possible to describe the implications of the energy transition robustly and in detail. In contrast to existing scenarios (Prognos, ko-Institut, Wuppertal-Institut, 2021), not just one transformation path was modelled, but a total of six target achievement scenarios. These target scenarios were clustered into four scenario groups, which differ in terms of the role of electrification, hydrogen and synthetic e-fuels as well as imports of renewable energy. The different technology orientations lead to different profiles in the transformation challenges: While greater electrification, for example, requires a faster transformation of end use, the increased use of hydrogen and e-fuels requires a more rapid ramp-up of the corresponding production capacities and infrastructures. In addition, different forms of import options are analysed due to the high economic and geopolitical relevance. On the one hand, the variety of models and scenario variations can be used to derive ranges of plausible developments and uncertainties. On the other hand, it is possible to estimate both the scope and critical bottlenecks of the energy transition, such as the minimum demand for electricity from renewable energies (RE) or the magnitude of necessary so-called negative emissions, i.e. CO2 removals from the atmosphere to offset unavoidable emissions." ;
    DC:acronym "Ariadne-2045" .

