        "carbon neutrality",
        "decarbonization pathways",
        "negative emissions" ;
    DC:abstract """The new Federal Climate Protection Act 1 (KSG) commits Germany to becoming greenhouse gas neutral ("net zero") in terms of anthropogenic emissions by 2045. After that, negative greenhouse gas emissions are to be achieved. Compared to the previous regulations, the new law stipulates a stricter climate protection target for the year 2030 and, more recently, legally binding annual reduction targets up to the year 2040. Both the achievement of greenhouse gas neutrality and the shortening of the transformation period represent a particular challenge compared to previous regulations. Compared to the greenhouse gas reduction targets pursued by the EU as part of the Green Deal, Germany's new statutory targets are significantly more ambitious, both in terms of quantitative reduction targets and the remaining period for action. 

Against this backdrop, the question arises as to ways and strategies to achieve these
new and significantly more ambitious targets can be achieved. It is also necessary to analyze whether the strategies pursued to date are still valid or whether they may need to be adapted. 

The greenhouse gas reduction scenario (KSG2045) analyzed in this study is based exclusively on the overarching reduction targets of the current Climate Protection Act. The analyses also include digressions, e.g. to be able to classify the robustness of the statements. The ETHOS (Energy Transformation Pathway Optimization Suite) model family, which was developed by the Institute for Techno-Economic Systems Analysis (IEK-3) at Forschungszentrum Jlich, is used for the analysis. This makes it possible to map the energy system on different scales in all its interactions and pathways. The spectrum ranges from detailed regional analyses of possible wind power and PV expansion to spatially resolved energy infrastructure analyses with hourly resolution. In addition, future global energy markets (e.g. synthetic fuels, hydrogen) can be simulated and possible energy imports and exports can be estimated in the context of the transformation process. It is also possible to carry out integrated infrastructure analyses that take all relevant energy sources (electricity, gas, hydrogen, heat) into account simultaneously.

The scenario presented below is a cost-optimal strategy. For this purpose, an energy system model was used that depicts the national energy supply from energy generation to sectoral energy consumption with a high level of detail. The model delivers a strategy at minimum cost, taking into account the exogenously set boundary conditions (e.g. compliance with greenhouse gas reduction targets, meeting energy-related demand). The special feature of the chosen approach, which follows a competitive cost logic, is that all interactions of the energy system are considered simultaneously. 

The analyses make it clear that in order to achieve the more stringent climate gas reduction targets and greenhouse gas neutrality, a far-reaching restructuring of the German energy supply is required that encompasses all sectors. Assuming the willingness to act and the acceptance of all stakeholders, it can be stated that the necessary transformation process is feasible from both a technical and an economic perspective. """ ;
    DC:acronym "FZJl-KSG2045" .

